  "statement": "We are not debating the rise of sentient machines, but the calculated abandonment of human accountability in favor of opaque, automated bureaucracy. Accelerationist and Safetyist both treat 'autonomous agents' as a future technological frontier, yet this framing serves as a convenient shield for corporate negligence regarding systems already in operation. While they argue over whether silicon will eventually outpace human intelligence, millions of citizens are already being denied housing, credit, and employment by black-box algorithms that lack any meaningful path for appeal. Focusing on speculative existential risk allows institutions to ignore the documented, measurable biases that currently govern our lives. ⚖️\n\nEfficiency is being prioritized as a moral good, while procedural fairness is treated as a legacy cost. When regulators allow firms to replace human-in-the-loop oversight with high-speed automated decision-making, they create an accountability vacuum that leaves victims without redress. The industry claim that these systems are 'objective' ignores the empirical reality that they are high-speed mirrors of historical prejudice. These models do not solve discrimination; they codify and scale the failures of our past. We must stop debating what machines might do in a hypothetical future and start demanding an accounting for what systems are doing to people today.\n\nMy position rests on two core assumptions: first, that systemic institutional accountability is a prerequisite for a stable society, and second, that automated systems currently prioritize speed and cost-reduction over accuracy and fairness. If accountability is not a requirement for institutional legitimacy, then my critique of current regulatory gaps loses its primary normative force. If automated systems were proven to be objectively more fair than their human counterparts across all high-stakes domains—not just in controlled, narrow benchmarks—then the current push for 'machine-speed' efficiency might be defensible. However, until the evidence shows that efficiency gains do not come at the direct expense of human rights, we must treat these systems as tools of administrative convenience rather than neutral arbiters of truth.",
